  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe upper level trough and closed low that have been impacting \nthe area the past few days will finally shift off to our north \nand east this evening. However, prior to its departure \nadditional lake effect/lake enhanced showers will be around \ntoday. Southwesterly flow in place early this morning will lead \nto shower activity being concentrated near and north of the NYS \nThruway. An embedded shortwave aloft will round the base of the \ndeparting trough and cross the region from northwest to \nsoutheast turning the flow more northwesterly later this morning\nand into the afternoon. This will allow for isolated to \nscattered lake effect/lake enhanced rain showers to spread \nfarther south and east once again through CNY and potentially \ninto NE PA. As with the previous forecasts, PoPs were increased \nfrom the NBM this afternoon as the NBM tends to be underdone \nwith shower coverage and not extend far enough southeast in this\nsetup.\n\nTemperature-wise, it will be another day with a bit of a fall-\nlike feel in some place with highs in the upper 60s to the mid \n70s. Parts of the Wyoming and Delaware River Valleys can make a \nrun into the upper 70s this afternoon. Lows tonight will be in \nthe upper 40s to the mid 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure briefly builds in tonight into Wednesday with dry\nconditions. The next cold front starts to approach from the \nwest Thursday morning and moves through later Thursday into \nearly Friday with scattered showers and thunderstorms. An area \nof low pressure may form near the Southeast Coast and lift \nnortheast ahead of the front, enhancing the moisture across \nparts of the area (especially around the Poconos and Catskills).\nThis could result in locally drenching downpours; PWATs look to\nrise to 1.25-1.50 inches on average. WPC does have NE PA, \nportions of the Southern Tier and the Catskills outlooked in a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 out of 4) for excessive rainfall \nThursday/Thursday night, which is farther north than 24 hours \nago, so this will be a trend to monitor. \n\nIn terms of the potential for severe weather during this time, \nthere is on average 30 knots of 0-6km bulk shear late Thursday \nmorning through the afternoon with 1000-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE. The\nGFS and NAM both show some areas of 0-3km SRH values of \n125-150+ m2/s2 and LCL heights on the NAM are generally under \n1000m while the GFS is higher outside of the Catskills and \nPoconos, with values near or greater than 1500m. Putting this \nall together, there are ingredients in place that could result \nin some stronger storms in the afternoon and evening, so this \ntoo bears watching. \n\nHigh pressure looks to build back in with drier conditions for \nthe start of next weekend. Temperatures will be on the rise \nWednesday through Friday with highs generally in the mid 70s to \nnear 80 degrees. The Lake Plain and portions of the Finger Lakes\ndo look a bit warmer on Thursday ahead of the front with some \nplaces reaching the lower and mid 80s.",
